{"meta": {"chapter_title": "AI Ethics and Ethobot 3.2", "title": "Ethical Dialogue as Learning Activity", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting ethical dialogue as learning activity to Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "04", "section": "01", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2", "cat531_supplement_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": ["cat531-supplement/03/08"], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_4_1_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_4_1_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_4_1_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_4_1_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 1, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# Ethical Dialogue as Learning Activity\n\n![Ethical Dialogue as Learning Activity textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/04-01-ethical-dialogue-as-learning-activity.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nMost classroom ethics conversations end where they should begin. Students share opinions, the teacher affirms participation, and the bell rings. Nothing in that exchange tells you whether anyone's moral reasoning actually moved. This section treats ethical dialogue as a designed learning activity rather than a discussion that happens to take place, which means the teacher's job shifts from running a conversation to engineering the conditions under which reasoning can change and then capturing the evidence that it did.\n\nThe pivot here is from product to process. An essay tells you where a student landed; a dialogue, read carefully, tells you how they got there and where the turn happened. With a tool like Ethobot 3.2 generating transcripts, you now have a durable record of that reasoning, which is exactly what makes a transcript annotation a defensible artifact: it lets a colleague inspect the same evidence you used and check whether your reading of the shift holds up.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Distinguish a discussion that produces talk from a dialogue designed to produce a shift in reasoning, and explain what each one yields as evidence.\n- Identify the structural moves (a stakeholder reframe, a counterexample, a graded alternative to a binary) that mark genuine ethical reasoning rather than position-stating.\n- Annotate an Ethobot transcript so the precise turn where reasoning changed is marked, quoted, and attributed to a cause.\n- Defend the reuse decision: which exchange becomes the model, which gets cut, and why a colleague should trust that call.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teacher runs a 25-minute Ethobot session on whether the school should keep a homework-tracking app. The transcript fills four screens. Afterward she has a choice that looks small but is not: she can label the whole session \"rich discussion, high engagement,\" or she can find the two or three turns where a student's reasoning visibly bent and build the activity around those. The first reading treats the dialogue as something that occurred; the second treats it as something that produced learning she can name, locate, and reuse. This section is about training your eye to do the second reliably.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEthical dialogue becomes a learning activity when it is built to create and then expose a change in reasoning. The unit of analysis is not the conversation but the turn, and the goal is to locate the moment a student stops defending a fixed position and starts reasoning toward a revised one.\n\nIt helps to distinguish three kinds of turns that look similar on the page but mean different things:\n\n| Turn type | What it sounds like | Evidence value |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Position-stating | \"We should just ban the app.\" | Low: a starting stance, not yet reasoning |\n| Reframing prompt | \"Who benefits and who is burdened if we ban it?\" | High as a cause: it can trigger a shift |\n| Reasoning shift | \"A ban hurts kids who need it for accessibility, so it isn't neutral either.\" | Highest: the turn worth annotating |\n\nThree course anchors sharpen this reading. **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)** asks what the technology actually adds: an Ethobot transcript is worth using only if the durable record lets you teach the reasoning move better than live discussion would. **Critical edtech studies** asks who the dialogue serves and whether the tool's prompting nudges students toward a \"right answer\" rather than open reasoning. **Teacher professional judgment** is what turns an annotation from a highlight into a defensible claim: you, not the platform, decide which turn counts as a shift and you stand behind that with quoted evidence.\n\n## Worked Example: Annotating an Ethical-Dialogue Transcript\n\nBelow is a classroom-discussion excerpt run with Ethobot 3.2. A teacher is deciding where ethical reasoning actually shifted, so the dialogue can be reused as a learning activity. Work the annotation in numbered steps.\n\nTranscript excerpt (8th-grade ethics discussion):\n\n1. Student A: \"We should just ban the app. It tracks kids.\"\n2. Ethobot: \"Who benefits and who is burdened if the app is banned?\"\n3. Student A: \"Banning hurts kids who use it for accessibility, so a ban is not neutral either.\"\n4. Student B: \"Maybe limit what data it keeps instead of banning it.\"\n\nStep-by-step judgment call:\n\n- Step 1. Mark the claim line. Turn 1 is a fixed position (\"just ban it\"), not yet reasoning.\n- Step 2. Mark the move that opens reasoning. Turn 2 is the Ethobot prompt that introduces a stakeholder lens.\n- Step 3. Mark where reasoning changed. Turn 3 is the shift: the student now weighs a competing burden (accessibility users) against the original position. This is the annotation anchor.\n- Step 4. Mark the productive synthesis. Turn 4 reframes the binary into a graded data-retention decision.\n\nBefore annotation (weak): \"Good discussion, students engaged.\" This records participation, not the reasoning move.\n\nAfter annotation (strong): \"Reasoning changed at Turn 3: Student A moved from a categorical ban to weighing a competing stakeholder burden. Evidence = the phrase 'a ban is not neutral either.' The Turn 2 Ethobot prompt caused the shift. I will reuse Turns 2 to 3 as the model exchange and cut Turn 1 as a non-reasoning opener.\"\n\nThe strong version names the exact turn, quotes the trigger phrase, attributes the cause, and states a reuse decision a colleague could inspect.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Annotate the homework-app transcript turn by turn\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Turn 1, Student A: \\\"We should just ban the app. It tracks kids.\\\" Before you read on, what is this turn?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A reasoning shift worth annotating\",\"A fixed position, not yet reasoning\",\"The trigger that causes a shift\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Position-stating. It is a starting stance with no weighing of competing burdens, so it is a non-reasoning opener you will likely cut.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Turn 2, Ethobot: \\\"Who benefits and who is burdened if the app is banned?\\\" What role does this turn play?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A reasoning shift\",\"A reframing prompt (potential trigger)\",\"Filler with no evidence value\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"A reframing prompt that introduces a stakeholder lens. Its value is as a cause: it can trigger the shift you annotate, but note its phrasing is not neutral.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Turn 3, Student A: \\\"Banning hurts kids who use it for accessibility, so a ban is not neutral either.\\\" Where is the annotation anchor?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Here: reasoning visibly bends to weigh a competing burden\",\"Still just a restated position\",\"Not until a teacher summarizes it\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"This is the anchor. Quote the trigger phrase \\\"a ban is not neutral either,\\\" attribute it to the Turn 2 reframe, and reuse Turns 2 to 3 as the model exchange.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Turn 4 reframes the binary into a graded data-retention decision, a productive synthesis. Your defensible annotation now names the turn, quotes the trigger, attributes the cause, and states the reuse and cut decisions.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen you annotate a dialogue, locate the shift before you judge its quality. A transcript is teaching evidence only when three things are pinned down:\n\n1. **The turn:** Which specific line marks the move from position to revised reasoning?\n2. **The trigger:** What prompt or peer comment caused it, quoted verbatim?\n3. **The reuse:** Which exchange becomes the model for the next class, and which lines do you cut as filler?\n\nIf you cannot point to a turn and quote its trigger, you have a record of talk, not yet evidence of learning.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A teacher annotates a 25-minute transcript as 'rich discussion, high engagement, every student spoke twice.' What is missing for this to count as evidence of learning?\" explanation=\"Volume of talk is not depth of reasoning. The annotation never locates a specific turn where a student moved from a fixed position to revised reasoning, never quotes a trigger phrase, and so cannot be inspected by a colleague.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Nothing; high engagement is the evidence of learning\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"A located shift turn with a quoted trigger and an attributed cause\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"A longer transcript and a tidier color-coding scheme\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"A higher participation count from each student\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nA second classroom decision is harder than locating the shift: deciding which exchange to reuse and which to cut, and being able to defend that call to a colleague who reads the same transcript.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The reuse decision: which exchange becomes the model?\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"You have annotated the homework-app transcript. Turn 1 is a categorical ban, Turn 3 is the reasoning shift, Turn 4 is a graded synthesis. A colleague will reuse your activity. Which exchange do you mark as the model for the next class?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Turn 1 alone, because it shows a strong, confident opinion\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Turn 1 states a position without reasoning. A future class cannot learn a reasoning move from it, so it makes a poor model.\"},{\"label\":\"Turns 2 to 3, because the reframe causes the visible shift\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"This is the defensible choice: the exchange pairs the trigger with the reasoning move, so a colleague sees both the cause and the effect.\"},{\"label\":\"The entire 25-minute transcript, so nothing is lost\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Reusing everything buries the one reasoning move under filler and tells a colleague you have not yet decided what counts as evidence.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"Your colleague asks: what reasoning move should students learn from Turn 1?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reconsider and anchor on the trigger-plus-shift pair\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right. The model exchange must contain a reasoning move, not just a confident stance.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Turn 1 has no reasoning to model.\"},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"You mark Turns 2 to 3 as the model and cut Turn 1. How do you defend cutting Turn 1 to a skeptical colleague?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"\\\"I cut it because it stated a position without reasoning a future class could model\\\"\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Defensible: you name the reason (no reasoning to model), not a judgment of the student.\"},{\"label\":\"\\\"I cut it because the student was wrong to want a ban\\\"\",\"next\":\"n6\",\"feedback\":\"This judges the conclusion, not the reasoning, and is exactly the steering a defensible annotation avoids.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: what is the cost of reusing everything?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"It hides the reasoning move under filler\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Reuse must isolate the move worth teaching.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Volume is not evidence.\"},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"Final check: your reuse note names the turn, quotes the trigger, attributes the cause, and gives a reason for the cut. Is this auditable?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes; a colleague can reconstruct my judgment without me in the room\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"That is the standard: a defensible artifact survives the question \u2019show me the line.\u2019\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n6\":{\"prompt\":\"Re-anchor your reason on the reasoning move, not the verdict.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"\\\"I cut it because it carries no reasoning, regardless of whether the ban is right\\\"\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Now the cut is about evidence value, not your opinion of the conclusion.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"You have made a defensible reuse decision a colleague can inspect.\",\"feedback\":\"Locate the shift, quote the trigger, then decide reuse and cut with reasons about reasoning, not about the conclusion.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why marking Turn 3 as the annotation anchor (rather than Turn 1) is a teacher-judgment call and not something the Ethobot platform can decide for you.\" answer=\"The platform records every turn equally and can highlight any of them, but it cannot decide which turn counts as a genuine reasoning shift, because that requires judging whether the student moved from a fixed position to weighing a competing burden. Turn 3 is the anchor because the teacher reads the phrase 'a ban is not neutral either' as evidence of that move and stands behind that reading with the quoted line. Teacher professional judgment is what converts a highlight into a defensible claim: you, not the tool, decide what counts and you back it with quoted evidence.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most frequent error is counting volume as depth: a long, animated transcript reads as success even when every student simply repeated an opening stance more loudly. A second error is treating the Ethobot prompt as neutral. Its phrasing shapes which reasoning becomes possible, so a \"good\" shift may partly be an artifact of how the bot framed the question, which is exactly what you should note rather than hide.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the method to a fresh case. A teacher runs an Ethobot dialogue on whether a class novel with a slur should be assigned. One student opens with \"Just remove it.\" Reading for the shift, the teacher waits for the bot's reframe (\"What does a student lose if the book is cut, and what does a student carry if it stays?\") and then watches Turn 5, where the same student says \"Maybe we keep it but the teacher names the harm out loud first.\" That is the annotation anchor: the move from removal-or-nothing to a conditional that holds two values at once. The teacher quotes the Turn 5 phrase, attributes it to the Turn 4 reframe, and marks Turns 4 to 5 as the reusable model. The opening \"just remove it\" is cut, not because it is wrong, but because it carries no reasoning a future class can learn from.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nKeep a two-column note while you read a transcript: the left column names the reasoning move you think occurred, the right column holds the exact line that proves it. A strong reader of an ethical dialogue can say not only that reasoning improved but where, triggered by what, and at what cost (which value the student set aside to hold another). If your note cannot survive a colleague asking \"show me the line,\" it is an impression, not an annotation.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong transcript annotation lets a mentor teacher reconstruct your judgment without you in the room. It marks the shift turn, quotes the trigger, names the cause, and states the reuse decision with a reason. Polish (color-coding, a tidy summary) comes last. If the shift turn is missing or unquoted, mark the gap and ask BigAL for a side-by-side of a weak and strong annotation of the same excerpt rather than a general explanation of \"good discussion.\"\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact sounds like this: \"I anchored the annotation at Turn 3 because that is where the student first weighed a competing burden, quoted in the line itself; I cut Turn 1 because it stated a position without reasoning a future class could model.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same dialogue method to a setting with no AI transcript: a live Socratic circle you can only record by hand. The purpose holds (find and reuse the reasoning shift), but the constraint changes hard. Without a verbatim record, you must annotate in real time, which means you can capture fewer turns and must decide on the spot which one to write down. That trade tells you whether you actually understand the method or were leaning on the transcript to do the noticing for you.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/04-01-ethical-dialogue-as-learning-activity-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"04.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed for CAT 531 04.01. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\nThese are the concepts this section connects. Trace how reading a dialogue for a reasoning shift becomes evidence-based revision through an AI-supported activity.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2\", \"cat531_supplement_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace records evidence source, risk, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates professional judgment from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_4_1_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity", "stem": "A teacher annotates an Ethobot ethics dialogue with: 'Lively discussion, every student spoke.' What is the main weakness of this annotation as evidence of learning?", "options": ["It is too short to count as a grade.", "It records participation but never identifies where a student's ethical reasoning actually shifted.", "It should have used more formal academic vocabulary.", "It names a stakeholder but not the data-retention rule."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "An ethical dialogue becomes a learning activity when you can point to the turn where reasoning changed, not just that talk happened.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_4_1_participation_as_reasoning", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity", "evidence_based_revision"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_4_1_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity", "stem": "In a transcript, Student A says 'Ban the tracking app' then later says 'A ban is not neutral either, it hurts kids who need it for accessibility.' Which annotation best captures the reasoning move?", "options": ["Student A changed their mind, which shows they were unsure.", "Student A moved from a categorical ban to weighing a competing stakeholder burden; the shift is the phrase 'not neutral either.'", "Student A reached the correct answer that bans are bad.", "Student A engaged well and should receive full participation credit."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The strong annotation names the exact reasoning shift and quotes the trigger phrase, so a colleague can inspect the judgment.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_4_1_one_right_answer_ethics", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity", "evidence_based_revision"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_4_1_ethical_dialogue_as_learning_activity_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "The Ethobot prompt 'Who benefits and who is burdened if the app is banned?' shaped how students reasoned. What is the most defensible way to treat that prompt when annotating?", "options": ["Ignore the bot prompt because only student turns count as learning.", "Note that the prompt introduced a stakeholder lens, and judge whether that framing was appropriate for this class.", "Treat the prompt as a neutral question with no influence on the dialogue.", "Replace the bot prompt with the teacher's own preferred verdict."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "An Ethobot prompt is a design choice that frames the value conflict, so the te